-- =====================================================================
-- Strive Elite - the College and Academy courses (the two routes)
-- =====================================================================
-- Coach Yinka, Oct 26 2026: "We need a college course and a academy
-- course." Two LESSON courses in the Recruiting shape: templates and
-- rules of thumb, no video. Recruiting stays the shared kit (CV, emails,
-- tape, tracker); College and Academy are the routes. Seeded SWITCHED
-- OFF for his read. No recruiting-rule dates (they change by division
-- and year; the pages say to ask us); never a roster, a scholarship or
-- an academy place promised. Idempotent; each block refuses a partial
-- course. The migration is the record of the text.
-- =====================================================================

do $$
declare
  cid uuid;
  placed int;
begin
  if exists (select 1 from public.elite_courses where slug = 'college') then
    return;
  end if;
  insert into public.elite_courses (slug, name, subtitle, intro, kind, active, sort)
  values ('college', 'College', 'The college route, step by step.', E'Grades open the door. Film gets you watched. The club coach''s phone call closes it.\nUse the templates in Recruiting for every email on this route.\nWhen a coach may reply to you depends on the division and the date. Ask us before you read a silence as a no.', 'lesson', false, 60)
  returning id into cid;
  insert into public.elite_course_steps (course_id, level_no, level_name, title, body, sort) values
    (cid, 1, 'Know the ladder', 'The levels', E'Division I, Division II, Division III, NAIA, and junior college. Every one of them is real college soccer with real coaches recruiting real players.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nDivision III gives no athletic money. It often gives academic money, and the soccer can be very good. Do not cross it off because of the name.\n\nJunior college is two years, then a transfer. For a late developer or a player whose grades need a reset, it is a route, not a step down.\n\nThe division is not the goal. The goal is a program where you get on the field in your first two years and a school you would stay at if soccer ended tomorrow.\n\nWatch two games of any program before you email them. If you cannot picture yourself in that team''s shape, move on.', 10),
    (cid, 1, 'Know the ladder', 'What a college coach checks', E'In this order. One weak link and the process stops there.\n\n1. GRADES. A player they cannot get admitted is not a player. Transcript first, every time.\n\n2. POSITION NEED. Are they losing a player in your position in your graduation year? If not, you are a backup plan at best.\n\n3. THE TAPE, THEN THE FULL GAME. The tape earns the click. The full game earns the phone call.\n\n4. THE PHONE CALL TO YOUR CLUB COACH. What kind of teammate you are. Whether you train. Whether your parents are a problem.\n\n5. SPEED AND SIZE FOR THE POSITION. Honest answer: this matters more the higher the division.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nIf you are aiming at Division I or II, register with the NCAA Eligibility Center during high school. Your school counselor knows which courses count. Do it early; it is paperwork, and paperwork late is a problem.\n\nTell your club coach which schools you are emailing. Ask them to expect the call.', 20),
    (cid, 2, 'The timeline', 'By grade', E'The route by grade. No dates, because the rules on when coaches may write to you change by division and year. Ask us for the current rules.\n\nGRADE 9\nGrades. Build the tape habit: film every game, keep the best clips in one folder. Start the list of twenty (see The school list). Go watch a college game.\n\nGRADE 10\nFirst emails go out, from the player. ID camps only at schools that replied or that you can picture playing for. Unofficial visits: any family can walk a campus and watch a training session. Tape updated after the season.\n\nGRADE 11\nThe heavy year. Showcases with the schedule sent a week out. Tape updated every season. Follow-ups on a rhythm. Narrow the twenty to eight by spring. Register with the Eligibility Center if you have not.\n\nGRADE 12\nDecide. Admissions deadlines are real and earlier than you think. Keep playing like someone is watching, because transfers and late offers happen every year.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nLate is fine. Plenty of players start this in grade 11 and land well. Late and disorganised is the problem.\n\nEverything above runs through the Recruiting templates. Same emails, same tracker.', 10),
    (cid, 3, 'The school list', 'Twenty across three levels', E'Five stretch. Ten fit. Five that would say yes today. Email all twenty.\n\nFOR EACH SCHOOL, ANSWER\n\nDo they recruit my position in my graduation year? (Look at the roster: how many in my position, what year.)\nCould I get in on grades alone?\nCould my family afford it without soccer money?\nWould I be happy here if I never played a minute?\nHave I watched them play?\n\nIf two answers are no, take it off the list.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nThe roster page is public. Count your position by graduation year before you send a single email. A team with four of you in the year above is not recruiting you.\n\nFit is not a consolation prize. The fit schools are where most players actually play.\n\nThe list changes. A reply moves a school up. Silence after two follow-ups moves it out. Twenty rows, always.', 10),
    (cid, 4, 'Camps and showcases', 'Rules of thumb', E'ID CAMPS\n\nGo only where a coach has replied to you, or where the staff of a school on your list is actually working the camp. Check the staff list; "guest coaches" is often one assistant.\n\nA camp is an evaluation, not a lesson. First ten minutes: simple, sharp, loud. Play your position. Nobody gets recruited for a trick.\n\nEmail a week before and the evening after. The templates are in Recruiting.\n\nSHOWCASES\n\nSend the schedule a week out to every coach on your list who is attending: field, time, number. Morning-of reminder, two lines.\n\nCoaches walk between fields with a list. If you are not on the list before the event, you are background.\n\nTHE MONEY TEST\n\nBefore paying for any camp or showcase, ask: which coaches on my list will be there, and have I emailed them? If the answer is none, keep the money.', 10),
    (cid, 5, 'The visit', 'What to ask a coach', E'Ask these out loud, the player asking, not the parent.\n\nWhere do you see me in your roster in year one? Year two?\nHow many players in my position are in my graduation year?\nWhat does a normal week look like in season and out of season?\nWhat happens academically if I am injured?\nWhat support is there for grades and scheduling?\nWhat is the money, year by year, and is it in writing?\nWho leaves this program, and why?\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nWatch a training session, not just a game. Training tells you how they coach.\n\nTalk to a current player without the coach in the room.\n\nAn answer you do not like now is a gift. An answer you do not like after you sign is a problem.', 10),
    (cid, 5, 'The visit', 'Money, plainly', E'Men''s college soccer money is usually partial. Academic money is often bigger than athletic money. Treat them as one number.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nAsk for the offer in writing, year by year. What it is, what it depends on, what happens if you are injured or cut.\n\nAsk what the full cost of attendance is, not the tuition line. Housing, food, travel, fees.\n\nNever choose a school on soccer money alone. Choose the school, then make the money work, then play.\n\nNobody can promise a roster spot or a scholarship. What the work earns is a clear path and a player ready for the moment.', 20);
  select count(*) into placed from public.elite_course_steps where course_id = cid;
  if placed <> 7 then
    raise exception 'College seed placed % of 7 pages', placed;
  end if;
end $$;


do $$
declare
  cid uuid;
  placed int;
begin
  if exists (select 1 from public.elite_courses where slug = 'academy') then
    return;
  end if;
  insert into public.elite_courses (slug, name, subtitle, intro, kind, active, sort)
  values ('academy', 'Academy', 'MLS NEXT, ECNL and the pro clubs.', E'Academies find players in games, not inboxes.\nYour current league is the filter. Climb one rung at a time.\nUse the Recruiting templates for every email on this route.', 'lesson', false, 70)
  returning id into cid;
  insert into public.elite_course_steps (course_id, level_no, level_name, title, body, sort) values
    (cid, 1, 'How they find you', 'Games, not inboxes', E'Academy and pathway clubs scout games and events. Your email is the reason someone comes to a game; the game is what gets you in.\n\nTHE LADDER AS WE SEE IT\nRec. Travel. ECNL RL or MLS NEXT 2. ECNL or MLS NEXT. Then the pro academies, which take most of their players from that top rung.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nClimb one rung at a time. A player who dominates a rung gets seen. A player who scrapes onto a rung and sits on the bench disappears.\n\nThe club coach''s phone call matters more here than anywhere. Directors call directors. Make sure yours knows where you want to go.\n\nBe the best player in your league games, not the best player at the one tryout. Tryouts confirm what scouts already saw.', 10),
    (cid, 2, 'The tryout', 'Email to a director', E'For an open tryout, an ID session, or to ask for a look. Sent by the player, with a parent copied for anything under 18.\n\nSUBJECT: [Birth year] [Position] | [Current club, league] | Tryout or ID session\n\nTEMPLATE\n\nCoach [Last name],\n\nMy name is [First Last], a [birth year] [position] with [club] in [league]. I am writing to ask about a tryout or ID session with [club or academy] for the [season] season.\n\nTape: [link]\nFull game: [link]\n\nMy next games: [date], [opponent], [venue], [time], number [number].\nMy current coach, [name], is happy to speak with you: [phone].\n\nThank you for your time.\n\n[First Last]\n[Birth year], [Position]\n[Parent name], [parent phone] (copied)\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nBirth year, not grade. Academies run on birth years.\n\nGive them a game to watch before you ask for a tryout. A scout who has seen you play arrives at the tryout already decided.\n\nUnder 150 words. Links do the talking.', 10),
    (cid, 2, 'The tryout', 'On the day', E'Everything a tryout decides, it decides early.\n\nBEFORE\nArrive early enough to warm up properly. Know your number and your group. One parent, quiet, on the far side.\n\nTHE FIRST TEN MINUTES\nSimple and sharp. First touch clean, pass to the right foot, talk. The coaches are sorting by then.\n\nTHROUGH THE SESSION\nPlay your position the way your club coach coaches it. Communicate in every drill. Lose a ball, win it back. No tricks nobody asked for.\n\nAFTER\nThank the coach who ran your group, by name. Email that evening: the After an ID camp template in Recruiting.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nThey are watching how you handle a bad touch more than the good ones.\n\nPlaying time in a team one rung down beats the bench one rung up. Film and development come from minutes.', 20),
    (cid, 3, 'The pro academy', 'How the MLS academies work', E'Most MLS academy players come from the clubs below them: the MLS NEXT teams, the affiliate and pathway clubs, and the academy''s own ID events. Being seen week after week in your league is the main route in.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nAsk the question early: which clubs feed the academy nearest us, and is mine one of them? Your club director knows.\n\nAn academy place changes school, travel and family time. Ask about all three before you ask about the shirt.\n\nCost differs by club. Ask plainly what the family pays and what the club covers. Get it in writing.\n\nNobody can promise an academy place. What the work earns is a clear path and a player ready for the moment.', 10),
    (cid, 4, 'Rules of thumb', 'The academy rules', E'AGE MATTERS EARLIER\nThe college route forgives a late start. The academy route mostly does not. If this is the target, the work is now, in the league you are in.\n\nGRADES STILL MATTER\nAcademies read report cards. A player with school problems is a problem they do not need.\n\nONE TAPE A SEASON\nSame rules as the Recruiting tape page. Position''s job, best clips first, full game underneath.\n\nWHEN AN OFFER COMES, ASK\nPlaying time expected. Training days and travel. School arrangement. Cost. What happens if it does not work out after a season.\n\nTHE PARENT''S JOB\nDrive, pay, proofread, stay on the far side of the field. Directors notice the parents who coach from the sideline, and not in a good way.', 10),
    (cid, 5, 'Overseas', 'Before you chase overseas', E'FIFA restricts international moves for players under 18, with narrow exceptions. A real overseas path almost always starts at 18 or through a family''s own circumstances abroad. Anyone promising a club overseas to a 15 year old is selling something.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nNever pay anyone up front for a trial, an agent, or an introduction. Real routes run through coaches who know coaches.\n\nA trial abroad is a trial, not an offer. Go with a return ticket and a plan for school.\n\nAsk us before you say yes to anything overseas. We have walked this road and we will tell you straight.', 10);
  select count(*) into placed from public.elite_course_steps where course_id = cid;
  if placed <> 6 then
    raise exception 'Academy seed placed % of 6 pages', placed;
  end if;
end $$;

